WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.412
Now apply the same design process to a bookstore.

00:00:03.412 --> 00:00:09.462
Start by identifying candidate entities such as customer, book, author, order, and order item.

00:00:09.462 --> 00:00:12.642
Then define one-row grain for each table.

00:00:12.642 --> 00:00:18.642
Think about the relationships. One customer can place many orders.

00:00:18.642 --> 00:00:20.862
One order can contain many books.

00:00:20.862 --> 00:00:26.942
One book can have several authors, which suggests another many-to-many relationship.

00:00:26.942 --> 00:00:33.742
Choose stable primary keys and protect important natural identifiers such as email or ISBN when appropriate.

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:40.482
Before revealing the sample direction, create your own simple design first.

00:00:40.482 --> 00:00:46.307
There can be more than one reasonable implementation, but every table should have a clear grain and every

00:00:46.307 --> 00:00:51.087
relationship should be explainable in plain language.

00:00:51.087 --> 00:00:54.362
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:54.362 --> 00:01:00.412
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:01:00.412 --> 00:01:04.862
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:01:04.862 --> 00:01:10.517
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:17.182
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:17.182 --> 00:01:23.967
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:26.250
not only whether the statement executes.
